🏢 Facade Design Pattern
📌 Definition

👉 Facade Pattern provides a simplified, unified interface to a complex subsystem.

In simple words:

Instead of talking to 10 different objects yourself, you talk to one object (Facade), and it coordinates everything.

🧠 Think Like a 10-Year-Old

Imagine you want to watch a movie at home.

Without a facade, you would have to:

Turn on TV
Turn on speakers
Turn on streaming device
Connect Wi-Fi
Open Netflix
Select HDMI
Adjust volume

😫 Too many steps.

Instead, you press one button:

Watch Movie

Behind the scenes, everything happens automatically.

That one button is the Facade.

🎯 Why Do We Need a Facade?

Suppose you have these classes:

DVDPlayer
Projector
SoundSystem
Lights
Screen

If the client directly controls them:

dvd.on();
projector.on();
screen.down();
lights.dim();
sound.on();
dvd.play();

Problems:

❌ Client knows too much

❌ Client becomes tightly coupled

❌ If subsystem changes, client changes

Instead:

homeTheater.watchMovie();

Much cleaner.

🎬 Real-World Example: Home Theater
Step 1: Subsystem Classes
TV
class TV {

    void on() {
        System.out.println("TV ON");
    }
}
Speaker
class Speaker {

    void on() {
        System.out.println("Speaker ON");
    }
}
Streaming Device
class StreamingDevice {

    void playMovie() {
        System.out.println("Playing Movie...");
    }
}
Step 2: Facade
class HomeTheaterFacade {

    private TV tv;
    private Speaker speaker;
    private StreamingDevice device;

    HomeTheaterFacade() {
        tv = new TV();
        speaker = new Speaker();
        device = new StreamingDevice();
    }

    public void watchMovie() {

        tv.on();
        speaker.on();
        device.playMovie();
    }
}
Step 3: Client
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        HomeTheaterFacade theater =
                new HomeTheaterFacade();

        theater.watchMovie();
    }
}
Output
TV ON
Speaker ON
Playing Movie...
🎯 Internal Flow
Client
   │
   ▼
HomeTheaterFacade
   │
   ├────────► TV
   ├────────► Speaker
   └────────► Streaming Device

The client talks to only one object.

