One call does everything.

🎯 Principle of Least Knowledge (Law of Demeter)
📌 Definition

👉

A class should know as little as possible about other classes.

In simple words:

Talk only to your close friends, not strangers.

🧠 Think Like a 10-Year-Old

Suppose your mother tells you:

"Tell your teacher I will be late."

You go directly to your teacher.

✅ Good.

Instead, imagine:

Mom
 ↓
Neighbor
 ↓
Bus Driver
 ↓
Principal
 ↓
Teacher

😵 Too many people involved.

The same idea applies in software.

🚨 Bad Example

Suppose:

class Car {

    Engine engine;

    Engine getEngine() {
        return engine;
    }
}

Client:

car.getEngine()
   .getPiston()
   .getCylinder()
   .start();

The client knows:

Engine
Piston
Cylinder

Too much knowledge.

If Engine changes:

Everything breaks.

✅ Better Design

Inside Car:

class Car {

    Engine engine;

    void start() {
        engine.start();
    }
}

Client:

car.start();

Much cleaner.

🎯 How Facade Helps Principle of Least Knowledge

Without Facade:

Client
  │
  ├── TV
  ├── Speaker
  ├── DVD
  ├── Lights
  ├── Projector

Client knows five different classes.

With Facade:

Client
   │
   ▼
Facade
   │
   ├── TV
   ├── Speaker
   ├── DVD
   ├── Lights

Client knows only:

Facade

That's exactly what the Principle of Least Knowledge recommends.

🔥 Why Is This Better?

Suppose tomorrow:

DVD removed

↓

Netflix added

Only:

HomeTheaterFacade

changes.

Client remains:

theater.watchMovie();

No changes.

🎯 Benefits of Facade

✅ Simplifies complex systems

✅ Reduces dependencies

✅ Loose coupling

✅ Better readability

✅ Easier maintenance

✅ Hides subsystem complexity

⚠️ Drawbacks

❌ Facade can become very large if it tries to do everything (sometimes called a "God Facade").

❌ Clients that need advanced subsystem features may still have to access subsystem classes directly.

🔥 Facade vs Adapter (Interview Question)
Facade	Adapter
Simplifies interface	Converts interface
Hides complexity	Makes incompatible interfaces compatible
Works with subsystem	Works with incompatible classes
Example: watchMovie()	Charger adapter
🔥 Facade vs Decorator
Facade	Decorator
Simplifies access	Adds functionality
New unified interface	Same interface with extra behavior
Doesn't change object behavior	Enhances object behavior
🎯 SOLID Principles Used

Facade naturally supports several SOLID ideas:

Single Responsibility Principle (SRP): The facade coordinates subsystem interactions, so the client doesn't have to.
Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP): The client depends on the facade instead of many concrete subsystem classes.
Open/Closed Principle (OCP): You can often extend or replace subsystem implementations with changes confined to the facade.
🎯 Interview One-Liner

Facade Pattern provides a simple interface to a complex subsystem, reducing coupling between the client and subsystem classes. It supports the Principle of Least Knowledge by ensuring the client communicates with only one object instead of many interconnected objects.

🧠 Memory Trick
Adapter = Translator (changes one interface into another)
Decorator = Enhancer (adds new behavior)
Facade = Reception Desk (one place to access many services)

Think of a hotel:

You don't call housekeeping, the kitchen, security, and laundry separately.
You call the front desk (facade), and it coordinates everything behind the scenes. That's exactly how the Facade Pattern simplifies interaction while keeping the client isolated from the complexity of the system.